<div class="text-center">
    <h2 class="heading text-center">5 Best Code Editors for Web Development</h2>
    <p>The Code editor is an optimized text editor focused on coding. It contains nece
        ssary features for coding, includes syntax highlighting, auto indentation, code assist, auto-com
        pletion, etc. A good code editor can increase the productivity level of developers to the next lev
        el. It can be standalone or it can be a part of an IDE. One important thing to keep in mind is that the
         code editor does not compile the code it only takes care of syntax. Many code editors are released every
          year. So it is very difficult for a newbie to choose the best code editor. So here is the list of the 5 
          best code editors that a programmer can choose to start his web-development journey.</p>
          <br>
          
</div>
<h4>
    <span class="fw-bold text-black small mb-1">1.Visual Studio Code
        </span>
    </h4>
    <img src="/images/notepad++.jpg" alt="Image"   class="img-fluid rounded mb-4">
    <div class="text-center">
        <p>It is a free source-code editor made by Microsoft for Windows, Linux and macOS. Features include 
            support for debugging, syntax highlighting, intelligent code completion, snippets, code refactoring, and embedded
             Git</p>
              <br>
              
    </div>
    <h4>
        <span class="fw-bold text-black small mb-1">2. Atom
            </span>
        </h4>
        <img src="/images/notepad++.jpg" alt="Image"   class="img-fluid rounded mb-4">
        <div class="text-center">
            <p>Atom is a free and open-source text and source code editor for macOS, Linux, and Microsoft Windows with support
                 for plug-ins written in JavaScript, 
                and embedded Git Control, developed by GitHub. Atom is a desktop application built using web technologies.</p>
                  <br>
                  
        </div>
        <h4>
            <span class="fw-bold text-black small mb-1">3.Sublime Text editor
                </span>
            </h4>
            <img src="/images/notepad++.jpg" alt="Image"   class="img-fluid rounded mb-4">
            <div class="text-center">
                <p>Sublime Text is a shareware cross-platform source code editor with a Python application programming interface. 
                    It natively supports many programming languages and markup languages, and functions
                     can be added by users with plugins, typically community-built and maintained under free-software licenses</p>
                      <br>                   
            </div>
            <h4>
                <span class="fw-bold text-black small mb-1">4.Light Table
                    </span>
                </h4>
                <img src="/images/notepad++.jpg" alt="Image"   class="img-fluid rounded mb-4">
                <div class="text-center">
                    <p>Light Table is an integrated development environment for software engineering developed by Chris Granger and Robert 
                        Attorri. It features real-time feedback allowing instant execution, debugging and access to documentation. 
                        The instant feedback provides an execution environment intended to help developing abstractions.</p>
                          <br> 
                </div>
                <h4>
                    <span class="fw-bold text-black small mb-1">5.Notepad++
                        </span>
                    </h4>
                    <img src="/images/notepad++.jpg" alt="Image"   class="img-fluid rounded mb-4">
                    <div class="text-center">
                        <p>Notepad++ is a text and source code editor for use with Microsoft Windows. It supports tabbed editing, which allows
                             working with multiple open files in a single window. 
                            The project's name comes from the C increment operator. Notepad++ is distributed as free software.</p>
                              <br>                           
                    </div>
